---
/**
 * One event row — the object `/schedule` draws for every open date.
 *
 * There is deliberately **no `variant` prop here** (D227): the row is one shape, and a second surface would differ
 * only in *how many* rows it prints and *what leads them*. The homepage did exactly that inside a preview card
 * until 2026-11-14 (D306), when `/schedule` became the only surface that prints a date; the reason for one row
 * component outlives the surface, because two components holding two copies of "what a date looks like" is how a
 * site ends up with two calendars that disagree.
 * `data/events.json` rather than in `src/content.json`.
 *
 * The composition (2026-11-07, operator: "the calendar event style on the home page is looking great... ensure
 * the events on the schedule page receive the same design improvement pass"): **date rail · copy · plate**,
 * right-to-left on `lg` and up, stacked below it. The homepage card that ruling was written against prints no
 * date since 2026-11-14 (D306), so this row is now the only thing that draws the composition; the ruling's
 * substance - one shape, one drawing - survives its second surface. The flip is at
 * `lg` (1024), not `sm` (640), and that is arithmetic the row cannot cheat: the leaf is 112px, the plate cap is
 * 323px, and the two `lg:gap-6` gaps are 48px, so a horizontal row needs 483px of the card's content box before
 * the copy lane gets a single character. Content-box widths @measured 2026-11-11 by
 * `node design/schedule-geometry.mjs --routes /schedule --widths 1240 1024 900 768 700 640`: shipped, the lane is
 * **633px at 1240** and **417 at 1024** - the two widths where the row is horizontal - and below `lg` it takes the
 * whole content box: **792 at 900**, **660 at 768**, **592 at 700**, **532 at 640**. The `sm` flip was measured as
 * the counterfactual: copy the build, move the flip, re-run the probe
 * (`cp -r dist/client .design/tmp-sm-flip && sed -i 's/lg:flex-row/sm:flex-row/' .design/tmp-sm-flip/schedule/index.html && node design/schedule-geometry.mjs --dist .design/tmp-sm-flip --routes /schedule --widths 900 800 700 640`)
 * and the lane collapses to **325px at 900**, **225 at 800**, **125 at 700**, **65 at 640** - under the 323px
 * plate at every width below 1024 - and the rows become **580x617** and **580x646** at 640 against the shipped
 * **580x509** and **580x480**: **+108px and +166px**, because a squeezed lane wraps every label into a column.
 * A 65px column is not a copy lane, and since S019/D272 put the sign-up links inside it, that is where an RSVP
 * label would wrap letter by letter. An earlier version of this comment quoted **49px at 640**, **109 at 700**
 * and **209 at 800**: those were arithmetic that subtracted the `lg:gap-6` 48px from widths where the markup's
 * `gap-4` (32px) is what actually applies, and the probe reads the DOM, so 49 is 65. Stacking is not a fallback here, it is the layout the
 * width can hold: it is the layout already measured at 390 and 320, and the gate measures 640 (stacked) and 1240
 * (horizontal), so both sides of the new flip are measured even though 1024 itself is not in the matrix.
 *   - `EventLeaf` is the date as a block, not as a sentence — the eye finds the day, then the month, and the
 *     weekday decides whether you can come.
 *   - the copy block is the sentence version of the same fact, which is what an assistive tech reads and
 *     what a visitor quoting the date on Discord copies; the leaf is `aria-hidden` so the date is said once.
 *   - PS-4 sits at the END of the row, at its cap, so the copy lane keeps its measure and the plate does not
 *     push the date off the left edge. Art on the right is also the reading order: date, then what it is,
 *     then the picture of it.
 *
 * What is a claim and where it comes from:
 *   - the date, the hour, the title, the venue line and the art all come from `upcoming()`, which reads the
 *     committed snapshot of the club's own Mobilizon feed (`data/events.json`). Nothing in this file is typed
 *     by hand.
 *   - a `STATUS:CANCELLED` row never reaches this component: `isUpcoming` drops it from the list while
 *     `/events.ics` still publishes it with its UID and a raised SEQUENCE, so a subscriber's client retires
 *     its copy and a person on the page is not told their plan is off (D223, D104).
 *   - an hour the organizer has not settled prints "time not set" rather than a guessed one (D169/D172);
 *     `asUpcomingItems` hands back `time: null` for exactly that case.
 *   - the quiet line under the title is the row's own `seriesNote`/`series`, never a frequency word — D92:
 *     the site publishes series names and real dates, and `check:content` fails "monthly" in visible text.
 *   - the venue line is a fact the organizer typed, carried verbatim in the feed's `venue.raw`, so it has a
 *     source and D92's bracket rule does not apply to it.
 *
 * Geometry, and why each piece is load-bearing:
 *   - `inset-shadow-rule` is the row's ONE edge (D220's `edges` half of the R6 pair). The leaf inside it is a
 *     `bg-tint` **fill** and not a second box — a keylined leaf inside a keylined row is two boundaries in
 *     one component, and `check:render` measures both sides of every ring and fails a keyline that straddles
 *     two grounds (D149-3c, design/records/0018).
 *   - the `<li>` is a flex container so `check:space` records the `data-slot="PS-4"` box at all: the survey
 *     only records a slot box for a child of a grid or flex container — measured 2026-11-03 for PS-5. Drop
 *     `flex` and PS-4 goes invisible to every design round that reads it.
 *   - `min-w-0` on the copy lane: without it a long venue string sets the lane's minimum width and the plate
 *     gets pushed out of the row instead of the text wrapping.
 *   - the spacing inside a row is the R6 `rows` option the operator ruled (D214, measured as a pair in D220),
 *     and it is the same spacing the removed homepage card used, which is what made the two surfaces read as one
 *     object while both existed (D306).
 *
 * "Visibly the same object" is measured, not intended: at 1240 the homepage's next-date card and the taller row
 * here are both **1164x217**, both carrying a **112x96** leaf, a **633x168** copy lane (the list's second row:
 * **633x136**, and a **633x164** lane inside its own **1164x196** box) and a **323x185** plate sitting 24px
 * inside the row's right keyline. At 390 the three boxes are 346x616, 346x560 and 346x504 with the same 112x96
 * leaf and a plate capped to the column (314x179 / 314x151). @measured 2026-11-11 by
 * `node design/schedule-geometry.mjs`, which prints those boxes for both routes. On the `lg` row the height is
 * the taller of the copy lane and the plate plus the row's 32px of padding; below `lg` the three stack: at 1024
 * the row is **948x224** with a **417x192** lane, at 900 it is **840x481** with a **792x136** lane and the plate
 * on its own line, at 800 **740x481** with a **692x136** lane, at 768 **708x481**, at 640 **580x509** with a
 * **532x164** lane. The breakpoint is a rule, not a preference: the row goes horizontal only when the lane left
 * beside the plate is at least as wide as the plate's cap - 417 ≥ 323 at 1024, while the `sm`-flip counterfactual
 * measures **225 < 323 at 800**, **193 < 323 at 768**, **125 < 323 at 700**, **65 < 323 at 640**. 900 is the one
 * width where the arithmetic would allow it (325 ≥ 323) and it is not a Tailwind breakpoint, so the row stacks
 * there at a measured **481px** against the horizontal counterfactual's **253px** - pricing a `md` flip with a
 * smaller plate cap is parked for the design round that measures the row at 900 (the render gate already prints
 * `FLIP WITH NO MEASURED WIDTH: lg(1024) md(768)`). @measured 2026-11-11 by the same probe.
 * The sign-up cluster costs **+44px of lane height** in every box, and that is not the same as row height: run
 * the same probe against a copy of the build with every cluster stripped
 * (`cp -r dist/client .design/tmp-no-cluster`, then remove the `[data-rsvp-cluster]` blocks), @measured 2026-11-11
 * by `node design/schedule-geometry.mjs --dist .design/tmp-no-cluster --routes / /schedule` - row height grows
 * **0px at 1240** on the card and the first row, where the plate is already taller than the lane, **8px** on the
 * row whose plate is short, **7-8px at 1024**, **44px at 900, 768, 700, 640 and 390** where the lane is the
 * tallest thing in the box, and **80px at 320** where the two labels wrap inside 244px.
 *
 * `data-copy` and `data-date` are probe handles, not styling. `design/schedule-geometry.mjs` measures the copy
 * lane inside the row; `scripts/check-schedule-window.mjs` reads `data-date` off the built page and compares the
 * dates `/schedule` actually printed against the window `src/lib/event-window.mjs` says it should print (D238).
 * The ISO date goes on the element rather than being parsed back out of the painted sentence because a gate that
 * re-parses prose breaks the day the prose is rewritten — and D173's rule is that a handle is part of the
 * interface, so it stays written.
 */
import { filled, formatDate, timeSpan } from '../lib/content.ts';
import type { UpcomingItem } from '../lib/content.ts';
import EventArt from './EventArt.astro';
import EventLeaf from './EventLeaf.astro';
import SignUpLinks from './SignUpLinks.astro';

type Props = {
  item: UpcomingItem;
};

const { item } = Astro.props;
---

<li class="rounded-card inset-shadow-rule px-4 py-4 sm:px-6 flex flex-col lg:flex-row lg:items-center gap-4 lg:gap-6" data-surface="row" data-date={item.date}>
  {/* The date rail (2026-11-14, D313, from the MS04 read). Before this the row stacked seven separate things:
     leaf, title, date sentence, series line, venue line, three links. Below `lg` the visitor met the date twice
     with the title in between - leaf above, sentence under the title - so the two drawings of one fact were three
     elements apart. The row is now three zones: this rail answers "when", the copy zone answers "what", the
     plate answers "what it looks like". The rail is the leaf alone, and that is a measured decision, not a
     shortcut: putting the sentence in the rail too (a 238px wide line beside a 112px leaf) leaves the copy lane
     507px at 1240 and **291px at 1024** - narrower than the 323px plate beside it, which is exactly the
     condition D273 exists to prevent @measured 2026-11-14 by `node design/schedule-geometry.mjs
     --widths 1240 1024`. So the sentence lives at the top of the copy zone, directly beside the leaf, and the
     lane keeps the width the probe measured. */}
  <EventLeaf date={item.date} />
  {/* `data-copy` is a probe handle for `design/schedule-geometry.mjs`, which measures the copy lane against
      the plate and the leaf; `min-w-0` is the load-bearing part (see the header). Inside it the seven parts are
      grouped as head (date sentence + title), prose (series + venue, capped at the measure) and the way out
      (the cluster). */}
  <div data-copy class="min-w-0 flex-1">
    <div data-head>
      {/* The class list on this span is exactly `text-label text-ink-3` and nothing more — the same handle the
          old homepage card wrote, so the probe that anchors on it still finds it (D173). It is the date said as
          a sentence, which is the version a screen reader hears (the leaf is aria-hidden) and the only place the
          hour appears at all. */}
      <p class="tabular-nums"><span class="text-label text-ink-3">{formatDate(item.date, 'wide')} · {timeSpan(item)}</span></p>
      {filled(item.title) && <h3 class="mt-1 text-lede font-bold text-ink font-body">{item.title}</h3>}
    </div>
    {/* `max-w-measure` (32em) is the MS04 copy-lane fix: uncapped, a row's prose ran the whole lane -
       633px at 1240, ~145 characters of PT Serif - past the 75-character reading band. `data-copy-prose` is
       the handle the probe reads the real characters-per-line off: `node design/line-length.mjs
       /schedule 1240 320 --no-build` (design/grill/line-length-measure.js counts a line's words with
       Range rects, so the number is measured, not estimated). */}
    <div data-copy-prose class="max-w-measure">
      {filled(item.series) && (
        <p class="mt-2 text-body leading-prose text-ink-2 font-body">{item.series}</p>
      )}
      {filled(item.venue) && (
        <p class="mt-2 text-body leading-prose text-ink-2 font-body">{item.venue}</p>
      )}
    </div>
    {/* S019/D272: the row's way out is now per-date rather than one group link, and every destination in it
        comes from `signUpPaths()` in `src/lib/rsvp.mjs` — the component does not choose the links, the
        resolver does, so `scripts/check-rsvp-page.mjs` can compare these bytes against that function.
        The feed's own per-event URL (`item.url`, the Mobilizon page for this date) is deliberately still unused
        here: D259 rules that a row links to the sign-up page, and Mobilizon is the club of record for dates,
        not for RSVPs (S017). Quiet links, not controls: D139 reserves `data-control` for the band's one
        primary, and a row of 48px buttons would out-shout every date in the band. */}
    <SignUpLinks item={item} />
  </div>
  {/* PS-4 (D190): the club's own event art, mirrored into src/assets/photos/ and resolved by filename, so
      adding art to a row is a file drop plus an `artFor` entry for its ratio and alt (src/lib/event-art.ts).
      The wrapper renders ONLY for a row with art, so a row without art keeps the geometry it had before this
      change byte for byte — a designed absence is the absence of a box, not an empty one (DESIGN.md "States").
      Capped at `.event-slot`'s 323px, the declared slot width, and never upscaled. Same cap as the homepage
      card, so the same poster is the same size on both surfaces. Re-measure with `npm run check:space`
      (`.design/tmp-whitespace/space-root-{1240,320}.json`) before quoting a plate size. */}
  <EventArt slug={item.slug} />
</li>
